as movie fans everywhere mourn the  passing of ray liotta it's only natural  to take a look back at his career and  what could have been  there's an interesting lost role in  particular that's worth exploring  here's the major superhero role ray  leota regretted turning down  rayoza is known for his work in gritty  crime dramas often playing an anti-hero  or a good cop gone bad  as far back as i can remember i always  wanted to be a gangster  the actor played a fictionalized version  of new york city mobster henry hill and  martin's course says he's critically  acclaimed goodfellas startup said kurt  russell's sociopathic police officer in  unlawful entry and stood out amongst the  star-studded cast in the sylvester  stallone led copland  but that doesn't represent the entirety  of his body of work before gaining a  reputation for his neo-noir roles the  actors starred in the action comedy  something wild and played the ghost of  famous baseball player shoeless joe  jackson in the kevin costner starring  fantasy drama field of dreams the  outsider has also lent his voice to  popular video games like grand theft  auto and won an emmy for his guest  appearance on the long-running prime  time medical drama er he had a venerable  career most actors would kill for but  it's always intriguing to consider what  might have been of all the roles he's  taken on for the big and small screens  there's one in particular that lyota  regrets not exploring playing batman has  become something of a badge of honor for  actors in the same way actors have taken  on iconic roles like hamlet and macbeth  others have portrayed the dark knight  over the years it's an exclusive club  and one that frequently entices debates  about who's the best batman of all time  interestingly enough lyota could have  been part of those conversations had he  chosen to take a meeting back in the  1980s  in a 2016 interview at the los angeles  times to talk about his work on nbc's  shades of blue  lyoto recounted the time his team  received a phone call from tim burton  who would go on to direct 1989's batman  in the mid-1980s burton gave the otas  agent a call while the actor was filming  something wild and asked if he would be  interested in both meeting him and  auditioning for the title role in his  then upcoming batman movie he recalled i  think tim burton is a great great  director and they always regretted not  going and meeting him just to talk  superhero films weren't widely popular  then and lyoto admitted that he thought  it was silly at the time the role  ultimately went to michael keaton who  had just starred in burton's beetlejuice  a year before and would reprise her role  in 1992 for batman returns  even though ray liotta wasn't too keen  on playing batman back in the 80s it was  still a highly coveted role  after all batman was already a household  name at that point and tim burton was  one of the most promising up-and-coming  directors suffice to say plenty of  actors likely auditioned for the role of  the caped crusader and fans loved  ruminating on what might have been had  the role guns of someone else other than  michael keaton what are you  i'm batman perhaps most intriguingly of  all william defoe was considered for the  part of bruce wayne given his eclectic  and provocative performances it would be  easy to believe that the foe would have  been better suited to play joker back in  the day but according to the actor in an  interview with mtv news he auditioned  for the dark knight it's unclear how far  along in the casting process to foe got  but we do know someone else in  contention for the part james bond actor  pierce brosnan according to brosnan  drawing a reddit ask me anything i went  and met with tim burton for the role of  batman but i just couldn't really take  it seriously any man who wears his  underpants outside his pants just cannot  be taken seriously that was my foolish  take on it it was a joke i thought but  how wrong was i these actors weren't  alone in their audition processes mel  gibson was reportedly also sought after  for the part but he had to turn it down  due to his commitments to lethal weapon  2. these are all many intriguing  what-ifs but ultimately it's perhaps  best to roll when to michael keaton  who's still considered by many to be the  definitive batman  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite celebrities are coming  soon subscribe to our youtube channel  and hit the bell so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]
 

